  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong Nor'easter will bring a prolonged period of \nrain, rough seas, strong winds, and coastal flooding/beach erosion \nstarting today and continuing through the weekend.  \n\nOur much anticipated nor'easter is on our doorstep this morning, \nexpected to bring increasingly gusty winds through the day ahead of \na prolonged stretch of wet and windy conditions through the weekend. \nNighttime microphysics satellite imagery early this morning shows \nthe disturbance sitting off the coast of the Carolinas and sending \nmoisture out ahead of it which will lead to increasingly cloudy \nskies as clouds thicken and lower today. The combination of this \nvery moist airmass (PWATs 1.5\"+), strong dynamics associated with \nthe deepening surface low pressure moving up the east coast, \nand our placement between it and a strong high pressure to our \nnorth will be the ingredients for an early season nor'easter. \nThis will be exacerbated by the duration of the storm as it \nstruggles to escape, being pulled back west toward New Jersey \nSaturday into Sunday. \n\nThe first concern is for strong to damaging northeast winds as the \npressure gradient tightens and a 55-65 kt LLJ shifts overhead NW of \nthe low center. Despite a westward shift in the track over the last \nfew days, the strongest winds are still expected to be experienced \nalong the coast, especially Cape Cod and the islands. Here, maximum \nwind gusts will be in the 45 to 55 mph range with 60 mph gusts \npossible at times, especially Friday night through Saturday morning. \nFurther inland, 30-45 mph gusts are more likely. Regardless, the \ntiming of this storm before trees have lost their leaves will \nexacerbate impact from broken limbs and increase the potential \nfor power outages. Strong winds will continue through Sunday but\nwon't be as strong as the storm weakens. \n\nAs for coastal flooding, the greatest concern is for the east coast \nof MA (especially south of Boston) where a few factors are at play. \nFirst, we've already dealt with days of gusty NE winds and this will \ncontinue through Sunday, increasing in magnitude shortly. This has \nsteadily increased nearshore wave heights which will help contribute \nto beach erosion. Finally, astronomical tides are increasing each \nday, so it will take less and less to see minor flooding impacts. \nThis being a long duration event (spanning several high tide cycles \nFriday night-Sunday) will not do us any favors either; in these \nsituations water often has trouble evacuating fully at low tide, \nfurther contributing to high water levels. A Coastal Flood Warning \nhas been issued for the east coast of MA for a combination of minor \nflooding with pockets of moderate, as well as expected widespread \nbeach erosion. Additionally, a Coastal Flood Advisory has been \nissued for the south coast of Massachusetts and Rhode Island where \nminor coastal flooding is possible due to high seas just offshore \nand increasing swell. \n\nFinally, this system will dump a lot of rain over the next 72 hours, \non the order of 3-5 inches thanks to the impressive moisture and \ndynamics given those previously mentioned PWATs and 925mb jet along \nwith convergence at the surface as winds turn from the SE to E \nto NE around the low. Latest ensemble probabilities continue to \nindicate the heaviest rainfall over eastern MA and RI. Flooding \nconcerns will be mitigated by the long duration of the event, \nthough pockets of urban and poor drainage flooding are possible.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure returns toward the middle of next week.\n\nWhen will we finally get a break from the unsettled weather? It \nlooks like it may be a slow improvement as the upper cutoff low \nisn't pulled into the steering flow and out of New England until \ncloser to Tuesday. Thus, while we won't see the wind and rain that \nwe saw over the weekend to start the work week, it will still be wet \nand unsettled. By Tuesday into mid week signs point to the return of \nmid level ridging and surface high pressure for a time.",
